## Hands-On Small Satellite Learning

The Satellite and Space Systems Division gives Sri Lankan undergraduates a fun, practical entry point into the world of satellite technology and space electronics.

You do not need an aerospace background to start. If you are curious about how satellites communicate with Earth, how weather data is gathered from orbit, or how microcontrollers survive high altitudes, you will fit right in.

## What We Do in Our Club

### 1. CanSat (Can-Sized Satellite) Workshops
A CanSat is a working simulation of a real satellite integrated inside the volume of a standard soft drink can:
- Soldering temperature, pressure, and air quality sensors onto custom breadboards and PCBs
- Programming microcontrollers to take sensor measurements every second during descent
- Designing miniature recovery parachutes to slow the CanSat down for a safe landing

### 2. High-Altitude Balloon Experiments
We explore atmospheric science by sending small student experiments up into the sky using latex weather balloons:
- Packaging lightweight camera modules and sensor boxes with foam insulation
- Logging temperature and atmospheric pressure drops as the balloon ascends
- Tracking the payload during its descent using GPS modules and radio signals

### 3. DIY Ground Stations and Satellite Tracking
Members learn how easy it is to listen to satellites orbiting directly above Sri Lanka:
- Building simple handheld Yagi-Uda and dipole antennas using PVC pipes and measuring tapes
- Using Software Defined Radio (SDR) USB dongles on laptops to decode live weather images from passing NOAA satellites
- Learning the basics of radio frequencies, antenna tuning, and orbital pass schedules

## What You Get as a Student Member

- Practical Electronics Skills: Learn soldering, circuit troubleshooting, microcontroller programming, and radio communication.
- Exciting Field Days: Participate in balloon launch days, antenna tracking sessions, and CanSat drop tests.
- Team Problem Solving: Work together to debug unexpected sensor glitches and celebrate successful data collection.
